I write:
>Better yet, how about adding actual sounds to the game?  A squeak when you
>step on a board, a zap for when a wand is used, misc./random sounds for
>battle (growls, swords clashing, etc.), mumbling for the reading of scrolls
>or spellbooks, etc.  You could have some real fun with these.  Just think of
>Atari's "Gauntlet".  Of course you'd have to make it an option, so that those
>without soundcards or those with bosses ;-) can play silently.

I just had some further thoughts on this.  Rather than hardcoding it into the
game, it might be better to patch it through a la chrhack.  Upon detecting a
certain game message or action, the sound program would play the corresponding
track.  There would be several advantages to this.  First, those who didn't
want the sounds could play the game without the extra loss of memory.  Also,
it would allow users to "customize" their sounds as in chrhack.  Sounds could
be exchanged via the net or other methods (e.g. shareware, public domain ware).
It might have to be a bit more versatile than chrhack, though; the more common
sounds could get annoying after a while.  (Who on Earth uses the "beep" sound
option currently in place??)  This could be accomplished by having several
sounds for each of the more popular actions, and having the program pick one
or more at random.  I also imagine a simple feature might be to have sound-
only, text-only (current silent version), or text and sound (where you'd get
the message _and_ hear the corresponding sound).
